# G098 — File Copy Utility

*v2. See `file-copier.md` for prose: three orthogonal concerns (traverse,
filter, policy, progress), overwrite policy as data (Skip/Overwrite/
RenameWithSuffix), filter as pure predicate, events as replay log,
rename cascades for collision resolution.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Export

[vault-export @src-root @dest-root
    IN ORDER TO
        copy a subtree of the vault to an export directory
        excluding tmp/cache, overwriting existing targets
        and persisting an audit log of every operation

    @fs     <- [fs[open {path: "~/Documents/Droplet-Org"}]]
    @events <- [fc[copy-tree
                     {fs: @fs,
                      src-root: @src-root,
                      dest-root: @dest-root,
                      policy: "overwrite",
                      filter: [fc[filter
                                   {include: [],
                                    exclude: [".tmp", ".cache"]}]]}]]
        || -> #copy-failed @resistance:reason

    @bytes <- [fc[total-size-copied {events: @events}]]
    [ui[render-progress {bytes: @bytes, events: @events}]]

    @log-lines <- @events mapped to [log[format-event {event: @e}]]
    [vault[save
            {path: ".vault/last-export.log",
             content: @log-lines joined by newline}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[fc
    new-fs
    add-file           {fs, path, size}
    add-dir            {fs, path}
    filter             {include, exclude}
    filter-allow-all
    copy-tree          {fs, src-root, dest-root, policy, filter}
                                                  ;; -> [CopyEvent] | error
    total-size-copied  {events}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: policy dispatched by `where` at the collision

[fc[copy-tree {..., policy: @p, ...}]
    IN ORDER TO
        visit every item in @src-root that @filter accepts
        and resolve each collision according to @p

    for each source-path walked
        where @filter does not accept -> skip silently (no event)
        where destination does not exist
            -> copy, emit #copied event
        where destination exists and @p is SKIP
            -> emit #skipped event, make no change
        where destination exists and @p is OVERWRITE
            -> replace, emit #copied event
        where destination exists and @p is RENAME_WITH_SUFFIX
            -> find first @dest-path + "." + n that does not exist
               (cascade n = 1, 2, 3, … until a free slot)
            -> copy there, emit #renamed event
]

Three orthogonal concerns (traversal, filter, policy) compose through
the copy-tree verb. Each concern changes without touching the others.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: events as data, not print or callback

[fc[copy-tree]
    IN ORDER TO
        return the operation's side effects as a value
        so the caller can sum bytes, audit, log, or replay freely

    events are returned as a list, NOT printed to stdout
    events are returned as a list, NOT delivered via callback
]

The caller chooses what to do with them: progress bar, audit trail,
idempotent retry, or discard entirely. Same discipline as Git's
reflog, Postgres WAL, Kafka — operation effects are first-class data.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @fs <- [fc[new-fs]]
    [fc[add-dir  {fs: @fs, path: "src"}]]
    [fc[add-file {fs: @fs, path: "src/a.txt",     size: 100}]]
    [fc[add-file {fs: @fs, path: "src/b.txt",     size: 200}]]
    [fc[add-dir  {fs: @fs, path: "src/sub"}]]
    [fc[add-file {fs: @fs, path: "src/sub/c.txt", size: 50}]]

    @events <- [fc[copy-tree
                     {fs: @fs, src-root: "src", dest-root: "dest",
                      policy: "overwrite",
                      filter: [fc[filter-allow-all]]}]]

    -> [fc[total-size-copied {events: @events}]]
    ;; -> 350
]
